---
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FaqAccordion from '@/components/FaqAccordion.astro'
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro'
import PageHeader from '@/components/PageHeader.astro'
import SetupCommands from '@/components/SetupCommands.astro'
import KeywordCannibalizationChecker from '@/components/tools/KeywordCannibalizationChecker.astro'
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import type { SchemaBreadcrumb } from '@/lib/schema'

const breadcrumbs: SchemaBreadcrumb[] = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools' },
  { label: 'Keyword cannibalization checker' },
]

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'What is keyword cannibalization?',
    answer:
      'Two or more pages on one site showing in Google for the same search, so they split the impressions and clicks between them. It is only a problem when the pages answer the same need. A product page and a buying guide can both deserve to show.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is this keyword cannibalization checker free?',
    answer:
      'Yes. It reads your own Search Console export in the browser, with no account, and the file is not uploaded.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can’t I use the zip from the Search Console Export button on its own?',
    answer:
      'That zip has a Queries table and a Pages table, but nothing that says which pages showed for which query. Cannibalization lives in that pairing, so the checker needs a query and page export from Search Analytics for Sheets or Looker Studio. The zip is still useful next to it, for each query’s own impressions.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I merge every page this lists?',
    answer:
      'No. The list shows where two pages share a query in Search Console. It cannot see what each page is for. Read both pages first, then merge, redirect or relink only when they answer the same question.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How is this different from the SEO Skill report?',
    answer:
      'The rules are the same. The report reads Search Console for you, so there is no export to make, and it checks the competing URLs live before you change anything.',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Free keyword cannibalization checker for Search Console"
  description="Find the searches where two or more of your pages share Google impressions. Ask your agent to read Search Console directly, or check a query and page export in your browser."
  canonicalPath="/tools/keyword-cannibalization-checker"
  {breadcrumbs}
>
  <main data-agent-content>
    <Breadcrumbs {breadcrumbs} />
    <PageHeader
      title="Keyword cannibalization checker"
      description="See which searches show more than one of your pages in Google, and how each page splits the impressions, from your own Search Console data."
    />

    <div class="docs-prose">
      <h2>Check with your agent, no export needed</h2>
      <p>
        The easiest way is to let SEO Skill read Search Console for you. It
        pulls which pages showed for each query straight from Search
        Console, up to 100,000 rows from the last 28 days of final data, so
        there is nothing to export. It also checks the top competing URLs
        live: a page that now errors shows before you spend time on it, and
        a page that now redirects counts as the page it points to.
      </p>
      <p>Add the hosted MCP server to your agent and sign in with Google:</p>
    </div>

    <SetupCommands id="cannibalization-setup" class="mt-6" />

    <div class="docs-prose mt-8">
      <p>Then ask:</p>
      <CodeBlock value="Which queries is example.com cannibalizing, and which page should own each one?" wrap />
      <p>
        In a terminal, install the CLI with <code>npm i -g seo</code>, run{' '}
        <code>seo start</code> to connect Search Console, then run the
        cannibalization section of the report:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock value="seo report --site example.com --section cannibalization" />
    </div>

    <div class="docs-prose mt-16">
      <h2>No account? Check an export in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The checker below uses the same rules on a file instead. It needs a
        CSV with a query and a page on each row, made in the steps under it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6">
      <KeywordCannibalizationChecker />
    </div>

    <div class="docs-prose mt-16">
      <h2>Export queries and pages for the checker</h2>
      <p>
        The Export button in Search Console keeps queries and pages in
        separate tables, so it cannot show two pages sharing one search. Get
        the pairs from the Search Console API with one of these free tools.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Search Analytics for Sheets.</strong> Install the{' '}
          <a href="https://searchanalyticsforsheets.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google Sheets add-on</a>,
          pick your site and the last 28 days, group by Query and Page, and
          request the data. Then choose File, Download, CSV.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Looker Studio.</strong> Add the{' '}
          <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs/studio/connect-to-search-console" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Search Console connector</a>{' '}
          with the URL Impression table. Make a table with Query and Landing
          Page, plus Url Clicks, Impressions and Average Position, then
          export it as CSV.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For each query’s own impressions, also export the same dates from
        Performance in Search Console and add that zip (or its Queries file)
        with the CSV. Without it, the checker adds up the page impressions,
        and one search that showed two of your pages counts twice.
      </p>

      <h2>How the checker decides two pages compete</h2>
      <p>
        It uses the same rules as the cannibalization section of the{' '}
        <a href="/docs/report">SEO report</a>, so the same rows give the same
        list of queries.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>A query needs at least 50 impressions over the export.</li>
        <li>
          A page counts as competing when it has at least 10 of the query’s
          impressions and at least a tenth of them. Below that it is a
          stray result, not a rival.
        </li>
        <li>
          When one page takes more than 80% of the competing impressions,
          Google has already picked it, and the query is left out.
        </li>
        <li>
          Searches for your own brand are left out, along with search
          operators and bare web addresses.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Queries with the most impressions and the most even split between
        pages come first. The page with the most clicks is marked as the
        first one to read, not as the one to keep.
      </p>

      <h2>Decide what to do with each query</h2>
      <p>
        Open every page in a row and ask whether a searcher would be happy
        landing on either. If yes, they compete: choose one, point your
        internal links at it, and merge or redirect the weaker page when it
        adds nothing. If they answer different needs (a category page and a
        how-to guide, say), keep both and make each page’s focus clearer in
        its title and headings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two pages sharing impressions is evidence of overlap, not proof of
        lost traffic. Check again a few weeks after a change, with the same
        date range, and compare.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="docs-prose mt-16">
      <h2>Keyword cannibalization questions</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <FaqAccordion id="keyword-cannibalization-faq" items={faq} />
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
